WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.320
Imagine training a robot controller by imitation.

00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.829
You watch the training loss, and it drops smoothly
and stays low.

00:00:07.909 --> 00:00:09.356
Everything looks fine.

00:00:09.436 --> 00:00:14.074
But if you actually deploy the policy and measure
how well it walks,

00:00:14.154 --> 00:00:18.791
the score jumps around violently from one training
step to the next.

00:00:18.871 --> 00:00:20.803
This paper is about that gap.

00:00:20.883 --> 00:00:24.272
The single-step imitation loss is calm and stable,

00:00:24.352 --> 00:00:30.031
yet the thing you truly care about, the
long-horizon reward, is oscillating wildly.

00:00:30.111 --> 00:00:35.096
And because it is expensive to roll out and
evaluate at every checkpoint,

00:00:35.176 --> 00:00:37.524
you usually never see these swings,

00:00:37.604 --> 00:00:42.450
which means whichever checkpoint you happen to grab
could be a bad one.
